## AUDIT 2 - Synthetic-feature validity (03_feature_validity.py) (PASS)
- Reproduction verification (original scope): SMOTE/ADASYN class counts exactly equal
  the saved 05_SMOTE/06_ADASYN class_distribution.json; X_res[:n_orig] == X_train
  exactly for both -> synthetic rows precisely isolated. PASS.
- Reproduction verification (corrected scope): corrected split matches the rerun's
  saved X_train (indices + values); class counts exactly equal the rerun's saved
  class_distribution_2015_2024.json for both methods. PASS.
- Strict per-feature validity (invalid = non-integer OR not in observed code set;
  [min,max] range alone NOT accepted as valid):
  SMOTE original: 1,318,498 synthetic rows, 0 rows (0.0%) with >=1 invalid feature.
  ADASYN original: 1,301,378 synthetic rows, 0 rows (0.0%) with >=1 invalid feature.
  SMOTE corrected: 1,210,810 synthetic rows, 0 rows (0.0%) with >=1 invalid feature.
  ADASYN corrected: 1,201,468 synthetic rows, 0 rows (0.0%) with >=1 invalid feature.
  All 15 features fully valid in all 4 audits. Affected features: NONE.
- MECHANISM FINDING (verified in imbalanced-learn 0.14.2 source):
  BaseSMOTE._generate_samples and ADASYN._fit_resample both end with
  `X_new = X_new.astype(X.dtype)` -> interpolated floats are SILENTLY TRUNCATED
  toward zero to int64, not rounded. Disclosed in report §11; not hidden, not
  "repaired". Impact negligible here: synthetic code distributions ≈ minority-train
  distributions (e.g. EXERANY2 0.612/0.388 -> 0.645/0.355); ~93% of SMOTE synthetic
  rows exactly duplicate a training profile (exact-duplicate kNN neighbors), which
  is why SMOTE ≈ ROS on this data.
- DECISION: no rounding/clipping applied (per hard rules); no algorithm change;
  SMOTE-N not implemented (assigned to another team member; unnecessary here since
  all codes are consecutive integers and the strict audit passes 0%). Documented in
  report §11.
- Saved: 12_Audit_Feature_Validity/{original_scope,corrected_scope}/
  (per-feature CSVs, validity_summaries.json, synthetic-samples parquet for both
  methods x both scopes).

